    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "property turnover and crime rates make sense, but those can be pretty noisy. i'm more curious about the \"community cohesion\" part. if we look at areas with higher transience or a lot of different demographics moving in and out, does that typically skew the risk profile higher in your experience? like, is there a detectable correlation between lower social cohesion in diverse urban areas and higher default rates?",
